Appendix A - Assignment Grading Rubrics
Use this appendix as your checklist. Each rubric includes what to submit, formatting rules, point breakdown, and credible resources you can use for help.
A1 - SQL + Decision Questions (Week 2)
Purpose: Translate decisions into data questions and answer them with SQL.
What to submit:
• A PDF (or DOCX) with 5 decision questions, 5 SQL queries, outputs (tables/screenshots), and 1–3 sentence interpretation per query.
• Your completed Week 2 notebook (Colab link) OR a PDF export of the notebook.
Formatting requirements:
• Label each question Q1–Q5 and each query SQL1–SQL5.
• Queries must run. If using Colab, include output cells.
• If you use AI tools, include a 2–4 sentence AI-use disclosure at the end.
Category	Points	Meets Expectations (B-range)	Exceeds Expectations (A-range)
Decision framing (actionable questions)	30	Questions are clear but slightly broad or missing who decides / what action changes.	Each question supports a specific action by a named decision-maker; includes what changes based on the answer.
SQL correctness	30	Most queries run; minor errors or inefficiencies.	All queries run cleanly; correct joins/filters/aggregations; results match questions.
Interpretation (output → decision)	25	Summarizes results but weak connection to action.	Explicitly links numbers to an action/recommendation; notes uncertainty/limits.
Clarity & organization	15	Readable but missing labels/formatting consistency.	Professional, easy to scan; clean tables; consistent structure.
Helpful resources (credible):
• SQLZoo: https://sqlzoo.net/
• Kaggle Intro to SQL: https://www.kaggle.com/learn/intro-to-sql
• Kaggle datasets: https://www.kaggle.com/datasets

A2 - Decision-Aligned Evaluation Memo (Week 4)
Purpose: Evaluate systems using decision outcomes and costs (not just accuracy).
What to submit:
• 1–2 page memo (PDF) describing: decision, error types, cost model, evaluation metrics, and an evaluation plan.
• One simple diagram or table mapping errors to costs (embedded is fine).
Formatting requirements:
• Use headings: Decision, Stakeholder, Error Types, Cost Model, Metrics, Plan.
• Cite at least 1 credible source (course text and/or NIST AI RMF).
Category	Points	Meets Expectations (B-range)	Exceeds Expectations (A-range)
Decision–cost alignment	40	Decision/costs identified but mapping incomplete.	Clear cost model; ties FP/FN (or failure types) to real impacts (time, money, safety, trust).
Evaluation logic	30	Uses at least one metric beyond accuracy; basic plan.	Metrics match decision risk (utility/cost-weighted, calibration, thresholding); explains why.
Clarity & structure	20	Readable but minor gaps.	Concise, professional; good use of tables/figures.
Evidence & citations	10	At least one credible citation.	Multiple credible citations; assumptions clearly labeled.
Helpful resources (credible):
• NIST AI RMF: https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework

A3 - MVP Build (Week 6)
Purpose: Build a minimal end-to-end informatics system that supports a decision.
What to submit:
• A runnable notebook (Colab link or .ipynb) demonstrating data → output end-to-end.
• A short operator playbook (½–1 page) describing how a human uses the output to decide.
Formatting requirements:
• Include a top cell: 'How to run this notebook'.
• If a step is manual, list it clearly and make it reproducible.
Category	Points	Meets Expectations (B-range)	Exceeds Expectations (A-range)
End-to-end functionality	40	Runs end-to-end with minor fragility.	Runs reliably; outputs repeatable; minimal friction.
Decision relevance	30	Output relates to decision; threshold/action unclear.	Clear action/threshold; rationale tied to cost/risk.
Explanation / playbook	20	Basic explanation included.	Practical instructions; includes failure mode + mitigation.
Code clarity	10	Readable but inconsistent.	Clean organization; functions; clear naming/comments.
Helpful resources (credible):
• OpenAI Cookbook: https://cookbook.openai.com/
• LangChain docs: https://docs.langchain.com/

A4 - Evaluation Harness (Week 10)
Purpose: Measure quality, identify failure modes, and propose mitigations.
What to submit:
• Notebook or script that runs evaluation on a small test set (≥10 tests).
• 1-page write-up of results + failure cases + mitigations.
Formatting requirements:
• RAG track: include grounding/citation checks. Predictive track: include calibration/threshold checks.
• Show at least 2 failure cases and what you’ll change.
Category	Points	Meets Expectations (B-range)	Exceeds Expectations (A-range)
Metric relevance	35	Metrics are reasonable but weak tie to decision risk.	Metrics map to decision risk + system failure modes.
Failure analysis	35	At least one failure case shown.	Systematic failure categorization; concrete mitigations + retest plan.
Clarity	20	Results are understandable.	Clear tables/charts; crisp conclusions.
Reproducibility	10	Mostly runnable; minor missing steps.	Fully runnable; clear instructions; documented variability.
Helpful resources (credible):
• OpenAI RAG explainer: https://help.openai.com/en/articles/8868588-retrieval-augmented-generation-rag-and-semantic-search-for-gpts
• Chroma docs: https://docs.trychroma.com/docs/overview/introduction

A5 - Causal Mini-Study (Week 12)
Purpose: Use causal reasoning to inform decisions when prediction is not enough.
What to submit:
• A short notebook or PDF with: causal question, assumptions, simple estimate attempt, and decision interpretation.
Formatting requirements:
• State treatment, outcome, and confounders.
• Include an 'assumptions' section and at least one validity threat.
Category	Points	Meets Expectations (B-range)	Exceeds Expectations (A-range)
Causal framing	40	Causal question present but variables/confounders unclear.	Clear intervention logic; confounders identified and explained.
Assumptions & validity threats	30	Some assumptions listed.	Explicit assumptions; realistic threats; suggests better data/design.
Interpretation for decisions	20	Summarizes result.	Explains what a decision-maker should do differently given estimate + uncertainty.
Clarity	10	Readable.	Clean structure; good visuals/tables.
Helpful resources (credible):
• DoWhy: https://www.pywhy.org/dowhy/

 
Appendix B - Project Milestone Rubrics and Detailed Instructions
